# A FIFTH memento slot, found 2026-08-15: a worker wrote to .claude/mementos/ rather
# than io/mementos/. Ignored for consistency — mementos are working state, never source.
# NOTE for the SessionStart resolver (register_session.py _memento_dirs): it scans four
# slots and NOT this one, so a record written here is invisible to a rehydrating seat.
.claude/mementos/
lupin-worktrees/

# DM traffic corpus — lives OUTSIDE the repo (Rick, 2026-08-13). The corpus is
# accumulating runtime data, not source: it belongs in <projects-data>/lupin/dm-corpus.
# These patterns are a BACKSTOP, not the mechanism — the writer resolves its path via
# $LUPIN_DM_CORPUS_DIR / fleet_data_root() and cannot write here. They exist so a
# hand-run harness that still writes into the tree cannot re-introduce a tracked corpus.
src/tmp/dm_traffic.jsonl
src/rnd/**/live-runs/*.jsonl

# Fleet data written INSIDE the repo by a NESTED-repo session — a BACKSTOP for a
# real defect, not a place anything is supposed to live. fleet_data_root() takes
# the sibling of the `projects` directory, which is correct for lupin itself
# (…/www.deepily.ai/projects-data/lupin) and wrong for a repo nested under it:
# a lupin-mobile session resolves …/projects/lupin/projects-data/lupin-mobile,
# i.e. into this working tree. Reproduced 2026-08-29 from both roots; the
# specimen was a Tiffany re-spin boot receipt. respin_wake_check.py's own header
# warns against exactly this ("never the repo root … no reader looks there",
# row 011f1f90) — so a file landing here is invisible to the readers that need
# it AND dirties this repo. The fix belongs in the resolver; this pattern only
# stops the debris from reaching a commit in the meantime.
projects-data/

# Tier run logs and coverage-gate scratch, at the repo root (Rachel, 2026-09-02).
# Ten of these were sitting untracked at session end and NOTHING ignored them:
# `git check-ignore` returned nothing for either pattern. A bulk `git add -A`
# would have swept ten run logs into a commit, and the only thing preventing
# that was the fleet's selective-staging habit — which is a rule that depends on
# remembering, and this repo's own position is that such a rule is not a control.
# These are run artifacts by construction: named for the sha they measured, and
# meaningless once that sha is no longer the tip.
.tier-*.log
.coverage-gate-*
